$body = <<<'HTML'
<div class="takeaways">
  <p class="takeaways__title">Key takeaways</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Most new roofs carry two separate warranties: a manufacturer's material warranty covering product defects and a contractor's workmanship warranty covering installation errors.</li>
    <li>"Lifetime" shingle warranties are almost always prorated after an initial full-coverage period, so their value drops over time.</li>
    <li>Hail, wind beyond the rated speed, poor ventilation and unauthorized repairs are the most common reasons a roofing warranty claim is denied.</li>
    <li>Register the warranty, keep your paperwork, and use qualified roofers for any later repairs to keep coverage intact.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<p class="lead">Residential roofing warranty coverage is layered, conditional and often misunderstood. A shingle bundle may say "limited lifetime warranty," but what that means depends on who installed the roof, how it was ventilated, whether the warranty was registered, and what actually caused the problem. This guide explains each type of residential roofing warranty, what it covers and what it excludes, so you know what protection you have before you need it.</p>

<p>At Austin Roofing we field warranty questions all the time from homeowners in Austin and Central Texas, usually after a leak or a hailstorm. Hail is the big one here: it is the most common reason Texas homeowners discover their warranty doesn't cover what they assumed it did. Read on before you sign a roofing contract or file a claim.</p>

<h2 id="h-the-two-main-types-of-residential-roofing-warranties">The Two Main Types of Residential Roofing Warranties</h2>
<p>Every roof replacement should come with documentation for two distinct warranties from two different parties.</p>

<h3 id="h-manufacturer-s-material-warranty">Manufacturer's Material Warranty</h3>
<p>The shingle or metal manufacturer warrants that its products are free from manufacturing defects for a stated term. For architectural asphalt shingles, that term is commonly marketed as "limited lifetime." Material warranties typically cover shingles and sometimes the manufacturer's own accessories (starter, ridge cap, underlayment) when they are installed together. They usually include separate, shorter coverage for wind (often tied to a rated speed, such as 110 or 130 mph with special installation) and for algae streaking.</p>
<p>What the manufacturer will not cover is how the product was installed. If shingles blow off because they were nailed too high, that is a workmanship problem, not a material defect.</p>

<h3 id="h-workmanship-warranty">Workmanship Warranty</h3>
<p>The roofing contractor issues a workmanship warranty covering errors in installation: improper nailing, missing or poorly lapped flashing, bad valley work, incorrect underlayment overlaps and similar mistakes. Terms vary widely from contractor to contractor, from one year to many years, so always get the term and conditions in writing. A workmanship warranty is only as reliable as the company behind it, so choose a local roofer you expect to still be in business years from now.</p>
<div class="callout callout--tip"><p><strong>Ask before you sign:</strong> "Who do I call if the roof leaks in year three, and what will that cost me?" The answer tells you more than the number of years printed on the brochure.</p></div>

<h2 id="h-what-residential-roofing-warranties-typically-cover">What Residential Roofing Warranties Typically Cover</h2>

<h3 id="h-manufacturing-defects">Manufacturing Defects</h3>
<p>Typical material defects include shingles that crack, blister, curl or lose granules prematurely because of a problem in how they were made, rather than weather or age. Defect claims usually require samples, photos and proof of purchase, and the manufacturer decides whether the product failed or something else caused the problem.</p>

<h3 id="h-installation-related-leaks-workmanship">Installation-Related Leaks (Workmanship)</h3>
<p>Leaks at flashing, valleys, pipe boots, skylights or wall junctions that trace back to installation are covered by the contractor's workmanship warranty during its term. This is where most real-world roof problems show up in the first few years after a replacement.</p>

<h3 id="h-limited-labor-coverage-certain-manufacturer-warranties">Limited Labor Coverage (Certain Manufacturer Warranties)</h3>
<p>A standard material warranty may cover replacement shingles but not the labor to tear off and reinstall them, and labor is the bigger expense. Some manufacturer warranties include labor during the initial non-prorated period. Enhanced warranties, available when a roof is installed as a complete system by a credentialed installer, may extend labor and tear-off coverage further. Read the labor section closely.</p>

<h2 id="h-common-exclusions-in-residential-roofing-warranties">Common Exclusions in Residential Roofing Warranties</h2>

<h3 id="h-storm-and-weather-damage">Storm and Weather Damage</h3>
<p>Hail is excluded from most standard shingle warranties, and wind beyond the rated speed is excluded too. Storm damage is a homeowners insurance matter. In Central Texas, where spring hail regularly moves through Williamson, Travis and Hays counties, this exclusion matters a lot. If a storm damages your roof, our <a href="/services/hail-damage-repair/">hail damage repair</a> and <a href="/services/roof-insurance-claims/">insurance documentation</a> services can help. You can also read about <a href="/blog/insurance-coverage-for-storm-damaged-roofs/">insurance coverage for storm-damaged roofs</a>.</p>

<h3 id="h-improper-ventilation">Improper Ventilation</h3>
<p>Nearly every shingle manufacturer requires attic ventilation that meets code or the manufacturer's own guidelines. An overheated attic, which is easy to end up with in an Austin summer, can bake shingles from beneath, and manufacturers may deny a claim if ventilation was inadequate. That is one of the best reasons to fix ventilation during a replacement. Our article on <a href="/blog/attic-ventilation-upgrade-benefits/">attic ventilation upgrade benefits</a> explains how to size it.</p>

<h3 id="h-unauthorized-repairs-or-modifications">Unauthorized Repairs or Modifications</h3>
<p>Installing a satellite dish, solar racking, a new vent or a patch with mismatched materials can void coverage on the affected area, or on the whole roof if the work was done improperly. Before you add rooftop equipment, ask your roofer how to do it without affecting the warranty.</p>

<h3 id="h-normal-wear-and-tear">Normal Wear and Tear</h3>
<p>Gradual granule loss, fading and the normal aging of materials under UV aren't defects. Neither is damage from tree limbs, foot traffic, pests or debris left in valleys. Oak leaves and cedar debris piled in a valley can hold moisture against the shingles, and the resulting damage counts as a maintenance issue.</p>

<h2 id="h-prorated-vs-non-prorated-warranty-coverage">Prorated vs. Non-Prorated Warranty Coverage</h2>

<h3 id="h-non-prorated-coverage">Non-Prorated Coverage</h3>
<p>During the initial period, often around the first 10 years for a standard shingle warranty, depending on the product, a valid defect claim is paid at full value for materials, and sometimes labor. This is when warranty coverage is worth the most.</p>

<h3 id="h-prorated-coverage">Prorated Coverage</h3>
<p>After that, the payout shrinks each year according to a schedule. Twenty years into a "lifetime" warranty, a defect claim may cover only a small fraction of material cost, with no labor. A long warranty term is not the same as full protection for that whole period.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
  <thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Non-prorated period</th><th>Prorated period</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Material reimbursement</td><td>Full replacement value</td><td>Declining percentage</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Labor</td><td>Sometimes included</td><td>Usually excluded</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Tear-off and disposal</td><td>Only on enhanced warranties</td><td>Rarely covered</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table></div>

<h2 id="h-extended-and-system-warranties">Extended and System Warranties</h2>
<p>Manufacturers offer upgraded "system" warranties that extend non-prorated coverage and add labor, and sometimes add workmanship coverage backed by the manufacturer.</p>

<h3 id="h-requirements-for-enhanced-warranties">Requirements for Enhanced Warranties</h3>
<ul class="checklist">
  <li>Installation by a contractor credentialed by that specific manufacturer.</li>
  <li>A minimum number of the manufacturer's matching components, such as underlayment, starter, hip and ridge, and ventilation.</li>
  <li>Registration within the manufacturer's deadline, sometimes with a fee.</li>
  <li>Installation that follows the manufacturer's written instructions exactly, including nailing pattern and ventilation.</li>
</ul>

<h3 id="h-what-extended-warranties-may-include">What Extended Warranties May Include</h3>
<p>Depending on the tier, an extended warranty may include longer non-prorated coverage, labor for tear-off and reinstallation, disposal costs and some workmanship coverage. Get the actual warranty document, not just a brochure, and confirm which tier your roof qualifies for.</p>

<h2 id="h-warranty-transferability-when-selling-a-home">Warranty Transferability When Selling a Home</h2>
<p>Many shingle warranties can be transferred once to a new owner, but the transfer often has to be completed within a set window after the sale, and coverage may shrink after the transfer. A transferable warranty helps resale in the Austin market, where buyers and their inspectors look closely at roof age. Keep the registration certificate with your closing documents.</p>

<h2 id="h-how-maintenance-affects-roofing-warranty-coverage">How Maintenance Affects Roofing Warranty Coverage</h2>
<p>Warranties assume reasonable care. Neglect that leads to damage, such as clogged gutters backing water under the drip edge, can give a manufacturer or contractor grounds to deny a claim.</p>

<h3 id="h-typical-maintenance-expectations">Typical Maintenance Expectations</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Keep valleys and gutters clear of leaves and cedar debris.</li>
  <li>Trim overhanging limbs, especially live oaks that rub shingles in high wind.</li>
  <li>Have the roof inspected after major hail or wind events. A <a href="/services/roof-inspection/">professional roof inspection</a> with photos creates a useful record.</li>
  <li>Address small issues like cracked pipe boots or lifted shingles promptly.</li>
  <li>Keep invoices and photos of any work done on the roof.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="h-filing-a-residential-roofing-warranty-claim">Filing a Residential Roofing Warranty Claim</h2>

<h3 id="h-steps-often-required">Steps Often Required</h3>
<ol class="steps">
  <li><h3>Document the problem</h3><p>Take dated photos of the damage inside and out, and limit further damage with a tarp if needed.</p></li>
  <li><h3>Call your installer first</h3><p>For leaks in the early years, the workmanship warranty is usually the right starting point.</p></li>
  <li><h3>Contact the manufacturer</h3><p>For suspected material defects, file with the manufacturer, providing proof of purchase, the registration number and photos.</p></li>
  <li><h3>Allow an inspection</h3><p>The manufacturer may request shingle samples or send a representative.</p></li>
  <li><h3>Don't repair before approval</h3><p>Apart from emergency protection, permanent repairs made before the claim is reviewed can complicate it.</p></li>
</ol>

<h2 id="h-why-professional-installation-is-critical-to-warranty-protection">Why Professional Installation Is Critical to Warranty Protection</h2>
<p>Most denied material claims come down to installation: wrong nail placement, missing starter strip, inadequate ventilation, or mixing components that aren't approved together. A roofer who follows the manufacturer's written instructions protects both warranties at once. Austin Roofing installs shingle and metal roofs to manufacturer specifications so the warranty stays intact. Learn more about our <a href="/services/roof-replacement/">roof replacement service</a>.</p>

<h2 id="h-choosing-roofing-materials-with-warranty-coverage-in-mind">Choosing Roofing Materials With Warranty Coverage in Mind</h2>
<p>When you compare products, look past the warranty headline:</p>
<ul>
  <li>How long is the non-prorated period?</li>
  <li>What wind speed is covered, and what installation does that rating require?</li>
  <li>Is labor included, and for how long?</li>
  <li>Is it transferable?</li>
  <li>Is there algae-resistance coverage? Central Texas humidity can cause black streaking on north-facing slopes.</li>
</ul>
<p>Class 4 impact-resistant shingles don't usually come with hail coverage, but many Texas insurers offer premium discounts for them, which can matter more in hail country. See our comparison of <a href="/blog/architectural-shingle-vs-asphalt-shingles/">architectural vs. 3-tab shingles</a>.</p>

<h3>Warranty Questions to Ask Before You Sign</h3>
<ul class="checklist">
  <li>Which manufacturer warranty tier will this roof qualify for, and who registers it?</li>
  <li>How long is your workmanship warranty, and is it in writing in the contract?</li>
  <li>Does the workmanship warranty cover the interior damage from a leak, or only the roof repair?</li>
  <li>Are all the components (underlayment, starter, ridge cap, vents) from the same manufacturer's system?</li>
  <li>Will the ventilation meet the manufacturer's requirements after the job?</li>
  <li>What paperwork will I receive when the job is complete?</li>
</ul>
<p>Keep everything in one folder: the signed contract, the invoice listing the exact product line, the warranty registration, and before-and-after photos. If you ever need to file a claim, or sell the house, that folder saves time and arguments.</p>

<h2 id="h-long-term-value-of-understanding-residential-roofing-warranties">The Long-Term Value of Understanding Your Roofing Warranty</h2>
<p>A warranty doesn't replace good installation, ventilation and maintenance. It works best alongside them. Know which warranty covers what, register it, keep your records, and use qualified roofers for any changes. If you want help reading a warranty or checking whether a problem might be covered, <a href="/contact-us/">contact Austin Roofing</a> for a free, no-pressure inspection.</p>
HTML;

